    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n\n'opfunc' receives only the motion type. Plugins also need the pre-operator cursor (for \"sticky cursor\", #41819) and the motion keys (future: `v:motion`, `i\"` vs `a\"`, #19426). When the opfunc runs the cursor is already at `'[`, so plugins stash it in the mapping's `<expr>` phase, which runs once per typed invocation. That doesn't work with dot-repeat / multicursor.\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nAdd new `v:` vars , similar to `v:operator` :\n\n- `v:operator_pos` start position: the cursor when the operator was typed.\n- `v:motion`: the motion keys as replayed (`iw`, `a\"`, `3j`). #19426 \n\n### Impl notes:\n\n- Can be \"derived\", does not require tracking new state. `v:motion` from the prepped redo (`redo_keys()`).\n- Start position from `oap->cursor_start`, saved already (internally) for `gq`/`gw`.\n- Can't use 'opfunc' params unless we want to special-case for Lua. A Vimscript `func Op(type)` which is not expecting 2 args, would raise E118.\n\n### Non-goals:\n- Observing motions after the action (already covered by `CmdAtom`).\n- Modifying a pending motion.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "From https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/19426#issuecomment-5295152689:\n\n> `v:motion` as described here is not implemented, but for observing textobject operations structurally, there is now a `CmdAtom` event\n\nI've actually tried using `CmdAtom`, but it was too \"asynchronous\", causing the cursor to briefly jump with large motions like `al`.\n\n> `v:operator_pos` start position: the cursor when the operator was typed.\n\nIf we want to prevent `operatorfunc` from jumping, `operator_pos` might not be enough. We would need something like `view` returned by `vim.fn.winsaview()`, especially for large motions.\n\nBtw, there is a workaround:\n\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<key>\", function()\n  local view = vim.fn.winsaveview()\n  vim.go.operatorfunc = function(motion)\n    vim.fn.winrestview(view)\n  end\n  vim.api.nvim_feedkeys(\"g@\", \"n\", true)\nend)\n```\n\nIdeally, I'd like a synchronous non-blocking API to get a motion, similar to `input()` (see https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/39719), which would be particularly useful for sequential operators (see [`MultiCursor.multi_operator`](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/discussions/41997), `gm<iw><al>`). It could return the motion and whether `operatorfunc` was cancelled. Currently, a cancelled `operatorfunc` is simply skipped, so the only workaround is to use events like `SafeState`.",
